WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.640
After years of waiting, Star Wars fans can
now officially see The Rise of Skywalker,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:11.099
the final chapter of an iconic franchise that's
spanned nine films and inspired several generations

00:00:11.099 --> 00:00:12.259
of movie-goers.

00:00:12.259 --> 00:00:16.460
But unfortunately for viewers in the United
Arab Emirates, there's a certain piece of

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.550
this sprawling galactic tale that they won't
be able to see.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:24.090
The United Arab Emirates has a population
of more than nine million, and is among several

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:30.160
areas within the surrounding regions where
members of the LGBTQ+ community have limited

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.480
or extremely restrictive rights.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.370
Homosexuality is officially illegal in several
countries within the Middle East, and as a

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:42.320
result, same-gender relationships are rarely,
if ever, portrayed in films.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.789
Several people who have seen The Rise of Skywalker
in Dubai, the United Arab Emirates' most populous

00:00:47.789 --> 00:00:52.260
city, have told The Hollywood Reporter that
they weren't able to watch the series' first

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.219
same-sex kiss.

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:59.909
Taking place at the end of the film and featuring
Commander Larma D'Acy and her wife, Resistance

00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:05.370
pilot Wrobie Tyce, the moment marks a historic
first for the Star Wars franchise, but one

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.670
completely removed for UAE viewers.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.750
This wouldn't mark the first time the UAE
and neighboring regions have censored a scene,

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:18.470
or even an entire movie, based on its representation
of LGBTQ+ identities.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:25.500
In 2013, a version of Wolf of Wall Street,
had around 45 minutes cut out entirely, essentially

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.270
rendering the moving unviewable for many who
tried to watch it.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:35.160
And in 2011, both Black Swan and Love and
Other Drugs were banned by the country's censors,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:40.950
while Ang Lee's Brokeback Mountain was banned
in the UAE in 2005 after a four-month review,

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.920
with the final decision made based on the
country's laws against homosexuality.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.420
Recently, Black Panther earned the distinction
of being the first film to screen theatrically

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.630
in Saudi Arabia after the country lifted a
35-year ban on movie screenings.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:02.610
However, a kiss between stars Chadwick Boseman
and Lupita Nyong'o was cut from its run in

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:07.720
the country, following a very difficult negotiation
during which Saudi Arabian officials requested

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:12.019
so much of the film be removed that Disney
was convinced it wouldn't have been worth

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:13.099
showing at all.

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:18.099
While the scene may have been removed from
the UAE run of The Rise of Skywalker, it surprisingly

00:02:18.099 --> 00:02:23.379
wasn't censored in China, which also has a
long history of eliminating on-screen portrayals

00:02:23.379 --> 00:02:26.400
of LGBTQ+ identity.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.300
Reports from movie attendees in both Beijing
and Shanghai confirmed that moment had been

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:36.689
retained, though China had notoriously banned
Brokeback Mountain, and cut Michael Fassbender's

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:39.550
same-sex kiss from Alien: Covenant.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:44.180
But while some audiences around the world
who got to see the moment in Rise of Skywalker

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:49.769
moment celebrated it, others outright blasted
it, especially after director J.J. Abrams

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:54.359
had hinted at the importance of the moment
earlier, during the film's press tour, telling

00:02:54.359 --> 00:02:55.359
Variety,

00:02:55.359 --> 00:03:00.859
"In the case of the LGBTQ community, it was
important to me that people who go to see

00:03:00.859 --> 00:03:03.920
this movie feel that they're being represented
in the film."

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.670
But it wasn't quite the representation that
many fans had asked for.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:12.450
For years, a group of viewers had openly shared
their hopes of seeing the final trilogy's

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:17.580
leading men, Poe Dameron and Finn, spark a
canonical romantic connection.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:22.170
Actor Oscar Isaac revealed that while he had
no control over that particular character

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:26.999
development decision, he hoped for the films
to take Finn and Poe's relationship in a romantic

00:03:26.999 --> 00:03:27.999
direction.

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:32.409
John Boyega also spurred fan interest in a
relationship between the two characters before

00:03:32.409 --> 00:03:38.029
the latest film's debut, sharing a cheeky
Instagram video in which he penned a long-winded,

00:03:38.029 --> 00:03:40.569
romantic letter to his "dearest Oscar."

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:45.980
"Bro, I think, lo-key, sometimes I feel BB-8
is straight up is jealous of our s---."

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:51.109
While fans may not have gotten the exact romantic
relationship they were hoping for, many have

00:03:51.109 --> 00:03:54.800
excitedly come out in support of the on-screen
moment they were given, with just as many

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.230
fans wishing that the moment lasted longer,
or wasn't confined to background characters.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:06.170
In an industry where LGBTQ+ representation
isn't internationally accepted, what's clear

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:10.939
from the fan response and the UAE's decision
to cut the historic moment is that the film

00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:15.279
was never going to please The Rise of Skywalker's
entire audience.

